Behaviorally, water ouzels are often seen near fast-flowing streams and rivers, where they forage for insects and small invertebrates on the rocks. They are known to dive into the water to catch prey, and their feet are partially webbed, aiding in swimming. They are territorial birds, and males are particularly aggressive towards intruders.

In terms of size, water ouzels are relatively small, measuring around 18-20 cm in length. Their proportions are compact, with a short neck and a robust body. 

The distinctive physical features, color patterns, and behavioral characteristics make the water ouzel easily identifiable among other similar species.
