In terms of size, American chameleons typically measure between 6-10 inches in length, including their tail. They have a relatively long neck and limbs, which allow them to move nimbly through branches and leaves. Their eyes are large and independently movable, giving them excellent depth perception and the ability to see in multiple directions simultaneously.

Behaviorally, American chameleons are known for their arboreal lifestyle, spending much of their time in trees and bushes. They are primarily active during the day and are excellent climbers, using their prehensile tails to grip branches and their sticky toe pads to adhere to surfaces. Chameleons are also known for their hunting abilities, using their long, forked tongue to catch insects and other prey.

