MLLM description: The red-breasted merganser is a distinctive waterfowl characterized by its long, slender bill, which is slightly upturned at the tip. Its plumage displays a striking color pattern: the head and neck are dark green or black, contrasting with the bright orange-red throat and breast. The body is predominantly grayish-brown, with white underparts. The wings have a mix of brown and white feathers, and the tail is short and rounded.

In terms of size and proportions, the red-breasted merganser is medium-sized, with a relatively long neck and a streamlined body adapted for swimming. Its bill is proportionally longer than that of many other duck species, aiding in catching fish and other aquatic prey.

Behaviorally, red-breasted mergansers are known for their diving abilities, often plunging into the water to catch fish. They are also skilled swimmers and can be found in various aquatic habitats, including rivers, lakes, and coastal areas.

Unique identifying traits include the combination of the long, slender bill, the bright orange-red throat and breast, and the contrasting dark green or black head and neck. These features set the red-breasted merganser apart from other similar waterfowl species.
