MLLM description: The Madagascar cat, also known as the ring-tailed lemur, is characterized by its distinctive black-and-white striped tail, which is one of its most recognizable features. Its body is covered in a mix of gray, brown, and white fur, with a black face and a white stripe running down the middle of its forehead. The lemur's eyes are large and expressive, often appearing to be staring directly at the camera.

In terms of size and proportions, the ring-tailed lemur is relatively small, with an average length of around 20-25 inches, including its tail. It has a slender build and long limbs, which allow it to move agilely through trees and branches.

Behaviorally, the ring-tailed lemur is highly social and is known for its loud calls and distinctive tail-wagging displays, which are used to communicate with other members of its group. They are also known for their acrobatic abilities, often leaping from branch to branch and even climbing upside down.

The unique identifying traits of the ring-tailed lemur include its distinctive tail, facial markings, and social behavior. These features make it easily distinguishable from other lemurs and primates.
