In terms of size, buckeyes are relatively small, typically ranging from about 1 to 2 inches in diameter. They are generally uniform in size within a single batch, though some natural variation may occur.

Buckeyes are not known for their mobility or behavior, as they are seeds and do not have the ability to move on their own. However, they are often found scattered on the ground, particularly after falling from trees during autumn.

The distinctive physical features of the buckeye, such as its textured shell and reddish-brown kernel, make it easily identifiable among other similar categories. Additionally, the size and shape of the buckeye are consistent across different batches, further aiding in its identification.
